    {
      "term": "Public welfare",
      "matchText": "Public welfare",
      "definition": "Ashoka's edicts (II and VII) cataloging the state-funded amenities he ordered along the empire's roads: dug wells, planted shade trees and mango orchards, and rest houses for travelers — framed not as displays of imperial power but as acts of compassion.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Moral officials",
      "matchText": "Moral officials",
      "definition": "The policy of Edict V, which created the Dhamma Mahamatras (\"Officers of Moral Conduct\") — a new class of officials charged not with taxes or troops but with promoting ethical behavior, mediating disputes, inspecting prisons, and looking after the welfare of women, the elderly, and the poor.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Reduced punishment",
      "matchText": "Reduced punishment",
      "definition": "Edict IV, in which Ashoka grants condemned prisoners a three-day reprieve to prepare for death, voices concern about the fairness of trials, and orders his officials to apply punishments with moderation — a pointed softening of the harsh penal regime the Arthashastra had prescribed.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
    {
      "term": "Frontier diplomacy",
      "matchText": "Frontier diplomacy",
      "definition": "The claim in Edicts II and XIII that Ashoka provided medical aid — herbal medicines and hospital access — not only inside his empire but to neighboring kingdoms, from the Tamil south to the Hellenistic Mediterranean: a ruler asserting responsibility for the welfare of people beyond his own borders.",
      "type": "concept"
    },
